WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.640
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, we spent a lot of time on writing code

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.240
with agents, but somebody still has to run the thing at three in the morning,

3
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.880
and that turned out to be where some of the most interesting stories were.

4
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.680
Let's first hear from a panel we put together at short notice

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.480
with Stéphane Jourdan, Simon Rohrer and Pini Reznik on what changes

6
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.240
when everybody in the company is shipping through an agent around the clock.

7
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.120
So now you have agents by everyone, from everyone doing things in production.

8
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.120
How do you know the impact of this 24 hours a day?

9
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:39.320
It's constant and you really need proactive agents and all the context.

10
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.800
What changed yesterday?

11
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.720
What was the relationship between this service and this other service?

12
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.720
How do you know that this service was actually connecting to the right thing?

13
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.800
But the variable was not.

14
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.880
The name was the IP, that sort of stuff.

15
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.320
So it's not only about like root cause analysis.

16
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.640
It's really about like the, the context, all the knowledge you can have from prod

17
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.680
your multiple accounts that all the stuff and agents today

18
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.520
you mentioned like DevOps is a bit back in,

19
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.640
in, in the space because the DevOps folks had a lot of tools, a lot of

20
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.600
data to work with the code, etc.

21
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.480
but for prod we really need to keep the lights up.

22
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.760
So yeah, that data and that context is your point

23
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.000
these days, including agents to fix this 24 hours a day.

24
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.240
Because even from a security perspective,

25
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.240
all the things that are being patched as well, constantly, you need to ensure

26
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.480
that the blast radius of things are still working after this.

27
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.040
So everything, everything is so is going so quickly these days.

28
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.400
We need — we need that data proactively.

29
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.560
Simon, I can see you're violently agreeing.

30
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.440
So I work in a regulated industry.

31
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.560
We can't quite move as fast, but we're still moving pretty fast. I,

32
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.840
like I said, I've been spearheading this and sort of coaching developers through.

33
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.840
And I think one of the things for me that surprised the developers or,

34
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.120
you know, we do "you build it, you run it".

35
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.480
So it is really developer operators.

36
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.040
The thing that has surprised

37
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.480
our developers the most is how useful the agents are for diagnosing production.

38
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.920
Yes, genuinely throw them at Elastic logs

39
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.480
plus ServiceNow incident reports, plus the code and the combination.

40
00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.600
Like you said, is this knowledge that the agents can have

41
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.760
they will solve an issue in 30 seconds

42
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.960
that could have taken developers hours

43
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.040
or maybe even days to fully understand this stuff is so powerful, you know,

44
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:37.920
and I think the people

45
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.800
who are just using it in the development context really should,

46
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.960
if they have that data available to them, like they say,

47
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.200
if they've got the good observability stack in place, should start to expand

48
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.280
there. They use it more broadly into this.

49
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.280
And I think, you know, we are living through a seismic shift.

50
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.920
We we don't know where we're going to end up.

51
00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.320
We don't know what the earthquake will do to us We also heard

52
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.040
from May Walter, co-founder of Hud, and hers is my favorite kind of talk.

53
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.800
One about something that went wrong.

54
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.200
They automated the investigation, shipped pull requests

55
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:16.320
for every high-impact fix, and discovered nobody wanted them.

56
00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.200
So they rebuilt the output to convince the human instead of the agent.

57
00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.560
So we said, okay, maybe we can just like add open

58
00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.720
pull requests for all of these high-impact, low-

59
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.600
risk changes that don't require migrations or anything super scary.

60
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.880
And we can just open a pull request.

61
00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.840
No, no, no, no, no,

62
00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.320
we don't want any pull requests that no one's going to go over

63
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.760
and we don't want to go over those 80 PRs even though they exist.

64
00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.080
And I think that's like if I if you can take one thing out of

65
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.240
this is like after you get it working and you want to automate it,

66
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.880
think about the scale and think about the humans that are still in the loop.

67
00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.640
We're not at the point where no one just cares about it.

68
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.160
And if you open a pull request, it's kind of like opening your Datadog

69
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.520
and Sentry with those like 700 issues that all they say is like, well,

70
00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.200
I'm not going to be able to fix this, so I'm not even going to try.

71
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.800
So we of course, we started with automated pull requests.

72
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.200
We realized no one cares about that.

73
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.560
And if it's not prioritized, then they're not going to do something about it.

74
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.760
And again, I am not complaining.

75
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.920
I'm not judging.

76
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.000
We're builders, right?

77
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.000
We have prioritization and it exists for a reason.

78
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.640
No one has time to just go over a bunch of pull requests built by the agent

79
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.960
that are statistically huge, and try to understand what happens.

80
00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.080
We don't want to own that.

81
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.200
We don't want to go into it.

82
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.200
So we actually need to convince the human that it's worth the attention.

83
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.040
Instead of convincing the agent

84
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.120
that it's worth the tokens, it will always think that they are.

85
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.560
So we map the hot paths, the endpoints

86
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.600
that are invoked often the business impact of that.

87
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.560
So is this impacting payments or authentication

88
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.920
or the set of things that you already know that you care about for your business?

89
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.600
And also the risk?

90
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.240
We are not looking for the best optimizations.

91
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.000
We are looking for the highest-impact, lowest-risk changes that can be done

92
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.880
so that we can go to the developer or the PM and say, hey, listen,

93
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:28.160
it's like that small and it's going to do like 30% improvement.

94
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.640
Amit Kushwaha is a principal solutions architect at NVIDIA,

95
00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.640
and he has a nice, counterintuitive finding.

96
00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.400
While your agent is off making a tool call, the GPU is sitting idle.

97
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.360
Account for that properly

98
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.600
and you can serve about twice as many users as your benchmark predicted.

99
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.720
The other thing that you need to think about.

100
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.720
So I have two colorful blocks here.

101
00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.120
Different rows are basically user,

102
00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.080
user one, user two, user three, user four.

103
00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:05.120
And what this slide is trying to convey is if you ignore the tools

104
00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:08.160
tool part of it, you're not really measuring the right

105
00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.880
workload again.

106
00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.680
So on the left side I'm assuming there is no tool call.

107
00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.640
So there is none of that purple block that I showed where the

108
00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.000
kind of things are running on the CPU.

109
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.840
In that case, what you ended up saying is user one, user

110
00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.000
two, user three, user four

111
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.240
are fully packing my elements or fully packing my GPUs.

112
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.200
So this whole block is basically when your large language model is being called.

113
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:36.760
But in

114
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.880
reality, that's not the case when you are running close.

115
00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.040
This is not how your GPUs look like.

116
00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.760
You're not continuously kind of keeping your GPUs busy.

117
00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:52.320
What is happening is you run your large language models and then actually

118
00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.320
you are running tool calls which are which are running on your CPU.

119
00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.360
So actually your GPU is not doing any work during that time.

120
00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.440
And depending on how big that variation

121
00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.120
is, you can actually support more users because you are effectively

122
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.240
not packing GPUs as much as you can.

123
00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.360
So just ignoring the concept that there is no tool actually ends up

124
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:21.240
predicting the wrong concurrency or wrong workload

125
00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.560
that that that you can support on your hardware.

126
00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.720
And there are some metrics there, like if you assume

127
00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:30.720
one call takes one second,

128
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.520
if you have a tool call that takes one second, two and half of your areas,

129
00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.760
you can actually support twice the number of users than you thought,

130
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.560
because a bunch of that work load is being done on CPU too.

131
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.160
And to close, Justin Cormack, formerly CTO at Docker.

132
00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.760
His single most useful

133
00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.920
debugging investment was getting the AI to build him a tracing framework

134
00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.400
Hand an agent a rare bug with no reproduction, and it guesses,

135
00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.680
Hand it a trace from an overnight run and it finds the thing

136
00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.760
I discovered that like even just getting the AI to build a

137
00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.880
hand-built, hand-maintained tracing framework was incredibly useful.

138
00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:14.920
You don't need to tie it into a production system or something,

139
00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.680
but anything that can give it traces that it can look at to debug

140
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.680
is amazingly useful.

141
00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:25.960
It in this case it had a bunch of overheads,

142
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.800
so when I used it for performance testing it was a little bit misleading.

143
00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.720
But it told it, you know, basically gave where the the big

144
00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.800
the big performance gaps were.

145
00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:43.320
And it was incredibly useful for debugging because I could give it,

146
00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.320
you know, I could run the test,

147
00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.640
I could have my test suites running, looking for race conditions or errors,

148
00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.560
give it a trace and say this happened overnight in my overnight run.

149
00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.080
We need to fix this.

150
00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:58.080
And it would

151
00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.600
it would let it actually lock down on

152
00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.000
what the real problem was rather than trying to guess.

153
00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.240
Because if you if you give an AI a bug

154
00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.000
but you don't know how to repro it, and it's a very it's a rare condition.

155
00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.160
It can waste a lot of time either.

156
00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.040
I mean, it can either fail to reproduce itself or it can guess what

157
00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:20.240
the solution might be and get it wrong or something.

158
00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.840
And if you can give it a trace and some trace tooling

159
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.680
and just get it to sit there and try and reproduce it

160
00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:32.920
and itself and see if it's the same thing, then it usually can.

161
00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.360
And that works really well.

162
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.360
So you don't need to necessarily hook it up to a production environment.

163
00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.360
You can really do this

164
00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:44.200
just by building, by getting the AI to build some tracing tools for you.

